%U A401694 61403596207,228780811288,855166423251,3205997991232,12051739556639,45416683422174,171544477096293,649321121741072,2462621483636759
%N A401694 Number of ways to apply one of four moves (see Comments) to a nonempty Dyck word with a cut, where both sides of the cut have lengths at most n+1 and of the same parity as n+1 before the move and at most n after the move.
%C A401694 Writing | for the cut (which may be at either end), with x and y single letters and u and v possibly empty words, the four moves are the insertion u|v -> u(|)v, the shifts ux|v -> u|xv and u|yv -> uy|v, and the join ux|yv -> u'|v', where u' and v' have the same lengths as u and v, respectively, and u'v' is obtained from uv by making the partners of x and y a matched pair. Thus u'v' = uv if xy = )(, the partner of y changes from ) to ( if xy = ((, and the partner of x changes from ( to ) if xy = )). The join is not allowed if xy = (), which is the only case in which x and y are a matched pair.
%C A401694 The Dyck words with a cut in the name are those counted by A401693(n+1), and the results of the moves are among those counted by A401693(n). The four moves change the lengths of the prefix and the suffix by (+1, +1), (-1, +1), (+1, -1) and (-1, -1), so different moves of the same word with the same cut give different results. Thus a(n) is also the number of pairs (w, w') such that w is counted by A401693(n+1), w' is counted by A401693(n), and a move takes w to w'.
%C A401694 For fixed admissible lengths i and j of the prefix and the suffix, there are A000108((i+j)/2) Dyck words with such a cut. The insertion requires i <= n-1 and j <= n-1, the shift ux|v -> u|xv requires i >= 1 and j <= n-1, and the shift u|yv -> uy|v requires j >= 1 and i <= n-1. The join requires i >= 1 and j >= 1 and is not allowed for the A000108((i+j)/2-1) words with xy = (). Summing the numbers of allowed moves over all pairs (i, j) with 0 <= i <= n+1, 0 <= j <= n+1, i == j == n+1 (mod 2) and (i, j) != (0, 0) gives a(n).
%C A401694 In the transfer-matrix method described in A401693, moving the vertical line past one crossing changes the state by one of the four moves: the insertion starts a new pair of strands at the crossing, the shifts carry the strand next to the road to the other side, and the join connects the two strands next to the road. A join of a matched pair would close a loop, which is not allowed before the last crossing. Thus a(n) is the number of nonzero entries, all equal to 1, of the full transfer matrix from the states counted by A401693(n+1) to those counted by A401693(n).
%C A401694 In the mathematical transfer process, count one bucket for each allowed move from each distinct state, before contributions to the same new state are combined. In the step from n+1 to n crossings still to come, where a move is allowed only if its result has at most n strands on each side of the road, any set of states counted by A401693(n+1) therefore gives at most a(n) buckets. After the first crossing, every state at the start of such a step is one of these states; the first step, from the prescribed start states, gives fewer than a(n) buckets. Hogan's program matrixMeanders (see Links) lists a(1), ..., a(14) in its table totalBucketsMaximumBy_boundary.
%C A401694 It appears that, for each fixed n, a(n) is the maximum number of buckets in this transfer process in the step from n+1 to n crossings still to come, and that this maximum is reached if and only if N >= A007310(n+1) = 3*n + 1 + (n mod 2), where N is the total number of crossings. The conjecture concerns both A005316 and A000682, except that for A005316 with n = 1 the bound a(1) = 3 is already reached for N >= 4. This has been checked for N <= 43, which covers the threshold for n <= 14.
%H A401694 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401694/b401694.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A401694 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A401694 Iwan Jensen, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0008178">A transfer matrix approach to the enumeration of plane meanders</a>, arXiv:cond-mat/0008178 [cond-mat.stat-mech], 2000.
%H A401694 Iwan Jensen, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/0305-4470/33/34/301">A transfer matrix approach to the enumeration of plane meanders</a>, J. Phys. A: Math. Gen. 33 (34) (2000), 5953-5963.
%F A401694 a(n) = A000108(n+1) + Sum_{k=0..n} min(3*k - 1 + (n mod 2), 3*(n+1-k), floor(3*n/2))*A000108(k).
%F A401694 a(n) = A014140(n+1) + A014140(n) + A014140(n-1) - 2*(A014140(ceiling(n/2)) + A014140(floor(n/2)) + A014140(floor(n/2)-1)) for n >= 1, with A014140(-1) = 0.
%F A401694 a(n) = A401693(n-1) + A401693(n) + A401693(n+1) - 2*A014137(ceiling(n/2)) + 1 + (n mod 2) for n >= 2.
%F A401694 G.f.: (2*(1+x+x^3)*sqrt(1-4*x^2) - x*(1+x)*(1+x+x^2)*sqrt(1-4*x) - (1-x)*(2+3*x-x^2+x^3+2*x^4))/(2*x^3*(1-x)^2*(1+x)).
%F A401694 G.f.: ((1+x+x^2)*c(x)/(1-x)^2 - 2*(1+x+x^3)*c(x^2)/((1-x)*(1-x^2)) + 1)/x, where c(x) = (1-sqrt(1-4*x))/(2*x) is the g.f. of A000108.
%F A401694 D-finite with recurrence (n+2)*(n+3)*(1365*n^9-29298*n^8+248089*n^7-1009336*n^6+1647197*n^5+1621788*n^4-10855859*n^3+16228606*n^2-8924392*n+786240)*a(n) - (n+2)*(6825*n^10-134205*n^9+952835*n^8-2367961*n^7-4306859*n^6+37260897*n^5-72941935*n^4+22780981*n^3+90102094*n^2-95306112*n+19383840)*a(n-1) - (1365*n^11-30663*n^10+354865*n^9-2288787*n^8+7066003*n^7-2003681*n^6-48771397*n^5+124265247*n^4-77703268*n^3-103793956*n^2+157961232*n-48202560)*a(n-2) + (34125*n^11-684675*n^10+5216545*n^9-16425027*n^8-2718677*n^7+169060603*n^6-473977757*n^5+485613007*n^4+44933652*n^3-442858628*n^2+283880112*n-62354880)*a(n-3) - 2*(10920*n^11-224829*n^10+1733666*n^9-5378454*n^8-2248984*n^7+63589439*n^6-179092946*n^5+199164204*n^4-24868736*n^3-122229560*n^2+80784480*n-14666400)*a(n-4) - 4*(6825*n^11-141030*n^10+1133030*n^9-3990684*n^8+1581026*n^7+35103356*n^6-118889404*n^5+152178974*n^4-22682601*n^3-131939176*n^2+113777124*n-25280640)*a(n-5) + 8*(n-4)*(2*n-9)*(1365*n^9-17013*n^8+62845*n^7+21603*n^6-667648*n^5+1521978*n^4-926370*n^3-837648*n^2+1146888*n-285600)*a(n-6) = 0 for n >= 7.
%F A401694 a(n) ~ 7*2^(2*n+2)/(3*sqrt(Pi)*n^(3/2)).
%e A401694 a(1) = 3: writing | for the cut, the moves are the shifts ()| -> (|) and |() -> (|) and the join ()|() -> (|). The word ((|)) has no move.
%e A401694 a(2) = 12 = 1 + 3 + 3 + 5; by the type of move, the pairs are:
%e A401694   insertion: (|) -> ((|));
%e A401694   shift ux|v -> u|xv: (|) -> |(), (()|) -> ((|)), ()(|) -> ()|();
%e A401694   shift u|yv -> uy|v: (|) -> ()|, (|()) -> ((|)), (|)() -> ()|();
%e A401694   join: (|()) -> |(), (()|) -> ()|, (()|()) -> ((|)), (()|)() -> ()|(), ()(|()) -> ()|().
%e A401694 The words (((|))) and ()(|)() have no move.
%t A401694 a[n_] := CatalanNumber[n + 1] + Sum[Min[3 k - 1 + Mod[n, 2], 3 (n + 1 - k), Floor[3 n/2]] CatalanNumber[k], {k, 0, n}]; Array[a, 28]
%o A401694 (PARI) a(n) = binomial(2*n+2, n+1)/(n+2) + sum(k=0, n, vecmin([3*k-1+n%2, 3*(n+1-k), 3*n\2])*binomial(2*k, k)/(k+1));
%o A401694 (PARI) my(N=28, x='x+O('x^(N+3)), c=(1-sqrt(1-4*x))/(2*x)); Vec(((1+x+x^2)*c/(1-x)^2-2*(1+x+x^3)*subst(c, 'x, x^2)/((1-x)*(1-x^2))+1)/x) \\ via the g.f.
%o A401694 (Python)
%o A401694 def terms(N):
%o A401694     c = s = t = 1
%o A401694     T = [1]  # twice-summed Catalan numbers, A014140
%o A401694     for n in range(1, N+2):
%o A401694         c = c*(4*n-2)//(n+1)
%o A401694         s += c
%o A401694         t += s
%o A401694         T.append(t)
%o A401694     return [T[n+1] + T[n] + T[n-1]
%o A401694             - 2*(T[(n+1) >> 1] + T[n >> 1] + (T[(n >> 1)-1] if n > 1 else 0))
%o A401694             for n in range(1, N+1)]
%o A401694 print(terms(28))
